        <h2>Day Trips from Talpe</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/galle">Galle Fort</a> is the obvious priority — just 10km away and reachable in 15 minutes by tuk-tuk, the UNESCO-listed Dutch colonial fort is one of Sri Lanka's finest destinations for walking, eating and history. Budget a half-day minimum.</p>
        <p><a href="/places/unawatuna">Unawatuna</a> is only 3km away — close enough to walk along the coast road or take a quick tuk-tuk, and worth visiting for its famous crescent beach even if you prefer Talpe's quieter base. The Jungle Beach near Unawatuna is a further short walk and well worth it.</p>
        <p>The <strong>Koggala Sea Turtle Conservation Project</strong>, about 4km west near Koggala, runs guided visits to a turtle hatchery where eggs are protected and hatchlings released — one of the more thoughtful wildlife interactions on the south coast, best visited in the early morning. The <strong>Madu Ganga river safari</strong> (around 30km north near Balapitiya) takes visitors through an extraordinary mangrove river system by boat, past cinnamon islands and a temple on stilts — a half-day trip that makes an excellent contrast to the beach.</p>
        <p>For whale watching, boat trips depart from <a href="/places/mirissa">Mirissa</a>, about 35km east — most easily organised as a full-day tour from November to April.</p>

        <h2>Getting to Talpe</h2>
        <p>Talpe has its own station on the Colombo–Matara coastal railway — one of Sri Lanka's most scenic lines, which runs right along the shoreline through Talpe. From Colombo Fort, the journey takes approximately 3 hours; from Galle, it is just 10–15 minutes (one or two stops). The station is a short walk from the beach road.</p>
        <p>By tuk-tuk, Talpe is 15–20 minutes from Galle junction (LKR 500–700) and about 10 minutes from Unawatuna (LKR 300–400). From Colombo by car via the Southern Expressway, allow around 1 hour 50 minutes. Intercity buses on the A2 coastal road run frequently between Galle and Matara and stop near the village.</p>

